Master Design Decisions & Architecture Log


1. Data & Domain Rules

• Money Representation (Integer Minor Units):
	• Store and calculate all amounts as whole integers in the currency's smallest unit (e.g., 45000 kobo = ₦450.00, 1999 cents = $19.99), never floats. Always pair amounts with an explicit ISO currency code.
• Single Source of Truth for Balance:
	• Account balances are derived exclusively from transactions (sum of credits minus debits). Do not store or cache a balance field on the Account entity, so balance and transaction history can never drift out of sync.
• Append-Only Ledger & Reversals (v1 Scope):
	• Transactions are strictly immutable (no editing or deleting).
	• Mistakes are corrected only via a reversal—a new offsetting transaction linked to the original (leaving the original record intact). Each transaction can only be reversed once.
	• Reversals require the account to be active and have enough balance (current_balance - reversal_amount >= 0) so reversing income never creates a negative balance. Transfer reversals apply atomically to both active accounts under the same rule.
• Backdated Transactions & Account created_at:
	• account.created_at is stored in timezone-aware UTC (datetime.now(timezone.utc)) strictly as audit/sorting metadata.
	• Users can record backdated transactions dated before account.created_at (e.g., logging past receipts after creating an account), as long as account balance invariants hold.

2. Timezones & Validation Boundaries

• Explicit Timezone Rule (No "or"):
	• System audit timestamps (created_at): Always UTC (datetime.now(timezone.utc)).
	• User-facing calendar dates & "today" filters: Always resolved in the user's local timezone (e.g., Africa/Lagos), avoiding the 11:00 PM–12:00 AM UTC date mismatch.
• Separate Write Validation from Read Filters:
	• Writing a transaction: Reject future dates (date > today in local timezone).
	• Searching / reporting: Allow future end_date values so full calendar presets (like "Sept 1 to Sept 30" queried on Sept 15) work without erroring. Only reject inverted ranges (start_date > end_date).

3. Account Lifecycle & Invariants

• Active Balances vs. Visibility:
	• Main balance list: Shows only active accounts by default.
	• Reactivation & inspection: Dedicated Deactivated Accounts view lets users find, inspect, and reactivate closed accounts.
	• Transaction filters: Deactivated accounts remain selectable in transaction filters so historical reports stay complete.
• Zero-Balance Deactivation Rule (Enforced at Both Ends):
	• On deactivation: Verify derived balance equals 0.
	• On transaction create / reversal: Verify the target account(s) are active—block any new transactions or reversals on deactivated accounts so their balance stays 0.

4. Architecture, Layering & Naming Consistency

• Unidirectional Dependency Rule:
	• Core Domain (models/, exceptions.py): Zero outward dependencies. Account and Transaction own their own validation inside from_dict() / to_dict().
	• Services (services/): Holds business rules, balance calculations, and workflows; imports models/ and exceptions.py.
	• Storage / DAL (storage/): Imports only models/ and exceptions.py (never services/), calling from_dict() / to_dict() for persistence.
• Consistent Naming & Layout:
	• Use PEP 8 *Error naming for custom exceptions (e.g., StorageWriteError, not DatabaseWriteException).
	• Keep folder/module names identical across spec text and diagrams (e.g., ui/, services/, storage/, models/, exceptions.py).

5. Persistence, Scaling & Crash Durability

• Why JSON over SQLite (v1):
	• Human-readable for easy inspection/debugging, avoids SQL schema migrations while v1 evolves, and pairs directly with to_dict() / from_dict().
• Scaling Cap & Redesign Trigger:
	• Whole-file rewrite (O(n) per save) is capped at 5,000 transactions (~1–2 MB). Passing 5,000 transactions, seeing noticeable save latency, or adding multi-user access triggers swapping storage/ to SQLite.
• Atomic Writes & Crash Recovery:
	• Every save writes to .storage.json.tmp, calls file.flush() + os.fsync(file.fileno()), and atomically swaps via os.replace().
	• On startup, if a stale .storage.json.tmp exists from an interrupted write, log a warning and delete .storage.json.tmp, then load the intact .storage.json.

Core Design Checklist for Future Features

1. One source of truth: Never store a value you already derive from a ledger.
2. Protect invariants on all write paths: Guard both state transitions and underlying events.
3. Forgiving reads, strict writes: Never let query filters fail on valid calendar windows.
4. Dependencies point inward: Domain models and errors never import services or storage.
5. Document limits & recovery: State where your v1 approach breaks and what happens on crash.
